      "model_calls": 1,
      "phase": "synthesis",
      "plan": {
        "A": "Build on the fastest measured candidate (5.416 ms): retain compact adjacency, first-match ID resolution, preselected successors, and memoized iterative path evaluation. Test one isolated refinement, such as lazy successor selection cached only for nodes reached from requested roots versus eager preselection. Measure end-to-end and preserve input order, tie behavior, missing-ID handling, and float accumulation order.",
        "B": "Focus on ID lookup and adjacency-resolution costs as an independent experiment. Compare a lower-overhead lookup/resolution representation against the measured open-addressed-map candidate; the sorted-map variant was slightly slower (5.706 ms), while packed linear scanning and SIMD scanning measured 10.651 ms and 14.671 ms. Preserve first-input-match behavior for duplicate IDs and do not spend effort repeating the slower SIMD scan.",
        "C": "Investigate root-level parallelism only on top of the fast memoized engine. The prior OpenMP variants measured 10.212 ms and 11.819 ms, well behind the best serial candidate, so test whether a workload threshold and different scheduling can amortize overhead on the four-core ARM64 target. Keep serial execution for small workloads, avoid unsafe shared memoization, and retain request-order output.",
        "G": "Use the 5.416 ms A candidate as the measured starting point, not as proof that its changes combine additively with other proposals. Select only changes supported by correctness checks and measurements, then benchmark the exact integrated program. Preserve the source semantics: strict greater-than selection from an initial maximum of zero, first neighbor on ties, first matching duplicate ID, zero for missing roots, the -1 recursion sentinel, recursive-order float additions, original root tokens, and six-decimal output. Do not alter tests, oracle, compiler, budgets, target, permissions, or source constraints.",
        "analysis": "The baseline measured 12.027 ms. The strongest observed improvement is A's compact graph representation, one-time ID resolution and successor selection, and memoized iterative evaluation at 5.416 ms (about 2.22x baseline); a sorted-map alternative measured 5.706 ms. The measured packed-scan and OpenMP/SIMD variants were slower, so prioritize refining the winning sequential approach and treat parallelism as conditional. Measurements alone do not establish correctness: every final candidate and integrated result must remain correct for all valid inputs."
